Z||000006||Justin||08-15-2001||10:32 PM||||Well the truth is I am actually making the car for a game called Viper Racing, which closely shares the meshes of NFS 4.  But NFS 3 is the only game with a Fiero, so I need to convert it from NFS 3 to NFS 4.  This is simple, just import the file into a 3d mesh editer as a NFS 3 file (whatever that is) and export it as a file for NFS 4, the repack it into a NFS 4 .viv file.  I cannot convert directly from NFS 3 to Viper Racing, it is very complicated and would be a waste of time.  So I put it into a .viv file, unpack it, do some minor mesh editing, change the data format and recompile the car buy putting the meshes into .mod format and repacking everything into a .car, which can be directly inserted into the game.  In doing all this, I never really convert the car to NFS 4, just make the meshes NFS 4 format so they can be easily converted in a program called Zenoa Modeller (a 3d mesh editing program) and put into Viper Racing. So the Fiero won't work in NFS 4 really, unless I do some HEX editing to change the way the car shows it's data.  If I have time I wil do it and post it here.  But in the meantime, I could really use info for the 88 GT.  Thanks!<P>I didn't go into explaining all this in my first post because I knew it would confuse 90% of the people here.  It even confuses me sometimes  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>If at first you don't succeed, pretend you were never trying in the first place.<P>visit <A HREF="http://www.twinkiesproject.com" TARGET=_blank>www.twinkiesproject.com</A>  for information on the chemical properties, reactions, gravitational resonse, and density issues of a Twinkie.||||reg||1
